AnnexKit — Dual-license arrangement
====================================

This monorepo ships under two different OSS licenses, one per package:

  * sdk/        — MIT License           (see sdk/LICENSE)
  * backend/    — GNU AGPL-3.0          (see backend/LICENSE)
  * frontend/   — GNU AGPL-3.0          (when shipped; same as backend)
  * examples/   — MIT License           (matches the SDK they depend on)
  * docs/       — Creative Commons CC-BY-4.0
                  (the strategy document docs/ANNEXKIT_PLAN.md)

Why split:

The SDK is the developer-facing library a consumer pip-installs into
their codebase. Friction kills adoption for libraries, so it is MIT —
the broadest possible permissive license. Companies that import
``annexkit`` into proprietary apps face zero copyleft obligations.

The collector backend is the managed-service product. It is AGPL-3.0
to protect the open-core business model: anyone can self-host or
modify it, but if they expose modifications as a network service, they
must publish the source. This is the same split (and the same
reasoning) used by Sentry, PostHog, MinIO, and many other open-core
companies.

The dual license is intentional and stable. Reach out via
https://annexkit.dev if you need a commercial license that exempts a
specific deployment from AGPL obligations.

For the canonical license texts, see:
  * sdk/LICENSE
  * backend/LICENSE
